  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Fall apartment balcony decorating comes down to four things: seasonal plants, cozy textiles, warm lighting and a few rustic accents, all scaled to a small space and safe under your lease. You do not need much square footage to create a spot where you will actually want to drink coffee on a cool morning. Here is how to pull it together without drilling holes or breaking fire rules.</p>

<h2>Check the rules before you decorate</h2>
<p>Most apartment communities have balcony rules in the lease or community policies. Common ones include no open flames or grills, nothing hung over the railing that faces the street, weight limits, and no screws or nails in exterior walls. Fire rules vary by city, so confirm with your property manager and, if in doubt, the local fire marshal. Five minutes of checking can save you a lease violation notice.</p>

<h2>1. Start with seasonal plants</h2>
<p>Plants carry most of the fall look. Pick a few in deep orange, gold, burgundy and purple:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mums</strong> are the classic choice and come in nearly every fall color.</li>
<li><strong>Ornamental kale and cabbage</strong> add texture and handle cooler nights.</li>
<li><strong>Pansies and violas</strong> often bloom well through a Texas fall and mild winter.</li>
<li><strong>Ornamental peppers</strong> give bright pops of red and orange.</li>
</ul>
<p>Group pots at different heights for a fuller look. Railing planters and hanging baskets use vertical space so you still have room for a chair. A sturdy [[amz:balcony_planter|railing planter]] that hooks over the rail without hardware is ideal for renters.</p>

<h2>2. Layer cozy textiles</h2>
<p>Soft materials make a concrete balcony feel like a room. Lay down an outdoor rug in a warm plaid, rust or mustard tone, then add outdoor cushions and a throw blanket you can bring inside when it rains. An [[amz:outdoor_rug|outdoor rug]] made for weather resists fading and dries quickly, and it also hides a stained or worn balcony floor.</p>

<h2>3. Add warm lighting</h2>
<p>Lighting does more for the evening mood than anything else. Drape warm-white string lights along the railing or overhead, and add a couple of LED lanterns on the floor or a small table. Battery or solar options avoid running cords through your sliding door. Look for outdoor-rated [[amz:string_lights|string lights]] with warm, amber-toned bulbs to match the season.</p>
<!--products:outdoor_rug,string_lights,balcony_planter|Fall balcony essentials-->

<h2>4. Mimic a fire pit, safely</h2>
<p>Open flames, chimineas and many tabletop fire features are often prohibited on apartment balconies. You can still get the glow:</p>
<ul>
<li>Cluster flameless LED candles in a lantern or a bowl of river rocks.</li>
<li>Use a battery-powered faux fireplace or "flame effect" lantern.</li>
<li>Pair the glow with a heavy throw blanket and a warm drink.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Set flameless candles on a timer so they turn on at sunset. Your balcony will look lived-in and welcoming every evening without you remembering to flip a switch.</p></div>

<h2>5. Pumpkins, gourds and harvest accents</h2>
<p>Pumpkins are cheap, easy and instantly seasonal. Mix sizes and colors, from classic orange to white and pale green. Real pumpkins can attract squirrels and start to rot in warm Texas weather, so check them often or use faux pumpkins you can reuse next year.</p>
<p>A small wooden crate or two can serve as a side table or a riser for plants. A few corn stalks or a burlap table runner finish a harvest corner. Skip hay bales unless your community is fine with them; they are messy and can be a fire concern.</p>

<h2>6. Wreaths, garlands and metallic touches</h2>
<p>Hang a wreath of leaves, berries and pinecones on the inside of your balcony door or on a removable hook. Wrap faux leaf garland around the railing with zip ties, which come off cleanly. Add a few copper or brass accents, like a lantern or a planter, to catch the warm light.</p>

<h2>7. Make it smell like fall</h2>
<p>Scent is the finishing touch. A cinnamon or clove scented candle (flameless wax melts work too), a bowl of potpourri, or a small pot of rosemary all help. Keep anything fragrant away from where neighbors might find it overpowering.</p>

<h2>Budget-friendly ways to pull it together</h2>
<p>You do not need to buy everything at once. Start with the pieces you can reuse for years, like an outdoor rug, string lights and a good set of planters, then add seasonal items like mums and pumpkins each fall. Faux pumpkins, leaf garlands and a wreath can be packed into one storage bin after Thanksgiving and pulled out again next year, so your fall look gets fuller every season without starting from scratch.</p>

<h2>Keeping it safe and tidy through the season</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Issue</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Wind</td><td>Weight planters, secure garlands with zip ties, bring cushions in before storms</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rain</td><td>Use outdoor-rated fabrics and lights; store blankets indoors</td></tr>
<tr><td>Early freeze</td><td>Move tender plants inside or cover them overnight</td></tr>
<tr><td>Drips below</td><td>Use saucers under pots so water does not run onto neighbors</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>A small balcony is good practice for a bigger porch</h2>
<p>If you are renting while you save for or plan a home build, pay attention to what you love about your balcony. Do you use it most in the morning or evening? Do you want shade, privacy, room for a table? Those details matter when you design a porch or patio, and orientation is one of the factors in <a href="/energy-efficient-home-building-in-the-texas-heat/">energy-efficient home building in the Texas heat</a>. A west-facing porch with no shade is a very different space than an east-facing one. Our article on <a href="/crafting-comfort-and-joy-the-6-keys-to-a-great-home/">the 6 keys to a great home</a> covers how outdoor space fits the bigger picture.</p>

<h2>Next steps</h2>
<p>Pick a color palette, grab a few plants and a set of lights, and build from there. Want to turn that balcony into something greener year-round? Read our guide on <a href="/turning-an-apartment-balcony-into-a-mini-garden/">turning an apartment balcony into a mini garden</a>, and when you are ready to think bigger, start our <a href="/free-training/">free training</a>.</p>
